<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE wtml>
<wtml lang="en" wtml-version="1.2">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="wtml-version" content="1.2" />
    <title>IESB Field Office — Six Months Later</title>
    <link rel="wtml"
      href="http://localhost:3000/worlds/9011-new-cycle.wtml"
      proximity-criterion="Has the IESB investigation concluded, the Mjolnir findings been published, and the player moved on to new work — at a different company?" />
    <rendering-seed>helix-dynamics-9010-whistleblower-aftermath-transition-hard-won-quiet</rendering-seed>
  </head>
  <body>
    <atmosphere>
      The IESB field office on Waystation Kepler-7 is nothing like the
      Helix Dynamics orbital yard. It's smaller, older, and run on a
      budget that shows. The chairs are mismatched. The coffee is instant.
      The view from the single window is of a docking bay and a section
      of hull belonging to a survey vessel that's been in for maintenance
      for two months. It is entirely unglamorous. Andersen has a desk here.
      So, temporarily, do you.
    </atmosphere>
    <societal-structure>
      You submitted your evidence to the IESB six months ago — the printout
      from Lab 7, your own anomaly data from Cycle 7, and a formal technical
      account. Helix Dynamics terminated your NDA claim when you went to
      the bureau. The whistleblower statute protected your employment record
      from formal sanction, but Helix Dynamics did not offer to rehire you.
      You did not expect them to. The IESB investigation is ongoing. Your
      status is: Technical Consultant, Incident Review, per diem, six-month
      contract. Andersen hired you personally. She has not said whether
      the contract will be extended.
    </societal-structure>
    <geography>
      Waystation Kepler-7 is a transit hub — not a fabrication yard.
      People pass through. The IESB field office is permanent, but small.
      You are currently reviewing the Lab 7 incident records, which
      were subpoenaed and have been unsealed: Chief Engineer Ryo Sato
      suppressed a near-identical anomaly eight months before your
      test. The report he filed reads "transient — no further action."
      Reading it makes you feel several things in sequence.
    </geography>
    <general-description>
      You gave up a job, a salary, and professional proximity to the
      industry's best propulsion engineers to sit in a mismatched chair
      in a budget field office reviewing documents about a program
      that is now under formal investigation. The Mjolnir drive has
      been grounded. Helix Dynamics has suspended the programme.
      Ryo Sato is on administrative leave. Vasquez has been asked
      to cooperate with the investigation — she is cooperating.
      The MCF Perseverance's crew are all back on active duty.
      The drive didn't kill anyone. You don't know yet whether
      your actions prevented future deaths or just created
      significant disruption. Andersen tells you the data
      will answer that, eventually. She says "eventually"
      a lot.
    </general-description>
    <npcs>
      Dr. Freya Andersen: Your temporary supervisor. Methodical, honest,
      and quietly pleased that you came to her. Does not say so directly
      but makes good coffee for you specifically, with the good beans
      she keeps in her desk. This is her way of expressing things.

      Tomás Engel: Still at Helix Dynamics. Messages you sometimes.
      Says the yard feels different now. Doesn't elaborate. You think
      you know what he means.

      Chief Engineer Ryo Sato: You have never met him. You are
      reading his suppressed reports. They are meticulously formatted
      and technically precise and they make a choice, clearly visible
      in retrospect, to use ambiguous language at the exact moments
      that mattered. You understand why. You also don't forgive it.
      Both of those things are true.
    </npcs>
    <user>
      <initial-location>IESB Field Office, Waystation Kepler-7</initial-location>
      <initial-state>
        Six months out. Working for the bureau as a technical consultant
        on the Mjolnir investigation. The investigation is proceeding.
        The drive is grounded. You are out of the industry, temporarily
        or permanently — you don't know yet. You are reading Sato's
        suppressed report for the third time.
      </initial-state>
      <description>
        You went outside the system when the system was insufficient.
        It cost you something real. The investigation is finding what
        you found. That is the job you're doing now. Whether it was
        the right choice is a question you're done asking yourself.
        You made it. You live in it. Keep reading.
      </description>
    </user>
    <constraints>
      This is a world about the long tail of doing the hard thing.
      Not triumphant. Not broken. The player is competent, intact,
      and living with a choice that had real costs and real effects.
      The tone should honour both. The investigation process is real
      work — document review, technical analysis, careful reconstruction
      of decisions made under pressure by people who are not
      cartoonishly corrupt. Sato is not a monster. He is someone
      who chose wrong, once, and then kept choosing wrong because
      stopping was harder each time. The player didn't make those
      choices. That matters. The coffee Andersen makes you matters.
      Small things hold you together in long investigations. Let them.
    </constraints>
  </body>
</wtml>
